  "testProfile": {
    "$comment": "Test-Steckbrief (one line per changed test case; docs/05-analyse.md, 5d). All entries are regexes (Java syntax). errorCode: searched in the test itself - its annotations and its body, comments removed, string contents kept. edgeCode: searched only in the inputs of the code under test - the parameter sources (@ValueSource, @CsvSource, @NullSource, @EmptySource, @NullAndEmptySource, the it.each table, a Go test table) and the argument lists '(…)' of the calls to the test's targets (the changed code it calls directly, also inside assertions: expect(valid(null))) and of the 'inputCalls'; literals in fixtures, builders, setup and expected values do not count, and without a target call or parameter source edge comes only from the name. inputCalls: regexes that end at the '(' of a call whose arguments are inputs (HTTP requests: given().body(…), .put(…), http.post(…), fetch(…)). errorName / edgeName: searched in the test name and the names of its enclosing describe blocks, split into lower-case words ('rejectsInvalidNumber' -> 'rejects invalid number', 'TestCreate_Empty' -> 'test create empty'). The first hit decides, in this order: errorCode, errorName -> 'error'; edgeName, edgeCode -> 'edge'; else 'normal'. assertionKinds: every assertion statement of the test body (rules 'assertions') gets the first kind, in this order, with a matching pattern; 'existence' also takes every 'weakAssertions' match; no match = 'other'. mocks: collaborators replaced in the test, searched in the test and its surroundings (fields and annotations of the enclosing classes, setup blocks and code of the enclosing describe blocks, top-level code of the file; not other tests or sibling suites). A string is a regex with a named group: 'type' = the collaborator's name, 'ref' = a variable holding a mock (named after its declared type, else after the variable with a capital first letter), 'module' = a module path (its last segment). An object {pattern, name} adds the fixed name when the pattern occurs. Names lose mock/fake/stub/spy pre- and suffixes; the unit under test (from the test file name) is left out. levels: the first entry whose 'paths' (globs) match the test file, or one of whose 'code' patterns occurs in the surroundings while none of 'unless' does, sets the level; else 'unit'. A rules file without this section, or without one of its keys, uses the built-in value for that key.",
